inators so that integral is 0.5.\n\nThe overlap integral is:\n\u222b_0^2 h(t) [1 - h(t+k)] dt = \u222b g(t) [1 - g(t+k) - 1] dt ? No:\nh = 1-g, so \u222b h(t) [1 - h(t+k)] dt = \u222b (1-g)(t) [1 - (1-g)(t+k)] dt = \u222b (1-g(t)) * g(t+k) dt\n\nWe want to minimize this maximum over k.\n\nWe define g via its CDF. Let F_G(x) = \u222b_0^x g(t) dt, then F_G(2) = 0.5.\n\nThe function g must satisfy:\n- g(x) \u2265 0 on [0,2] (since h=1-g\u22650 implies g\u22641, so we need g(x) \u2264 1 but with integral 0.5)\nBut with M intervals, we'll distribute mass appropriately.\n\nWe'll use a simplex convolution to relate g(x) and g(t+k).\n\nLet:\nF_G(x) = p   if [x_j, x_{j+1}] is selected, etc.\n\nBut simpler: define g as piecewise linear between endpoints? Or constant in intervals.\n\nActually, to relate to the provided code, we'll consider the same discrete grid-based h.\n\nBut we need a continuous formulation. Let's stick to the discrete form for near identical gradient properties.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n- Start with M=16 intervals (determined by Euler clique number properties)\n- Define endpoints e = 2 * [0, b1, b2, ..., b_{M-1}]  (between 0 and 1 scaled to [0,2])\n- Define weights w (normalized \u2211w_i * (e_{i+1}-e_i) = 0.5)\n- Compute discrete counterparts at grid points\n- Evaluate C_5 using np.correlate\n- Use a covariance-adaptive evolution strategy with Bayesian updates\n- Repair invariants: ensure \u2211w_i * \u0394e_i = 0.5, and 0\u2264e0<e1<...<e_{M-1}<2\n\nThe evolution strategy:\n1. Initial population: simplex from uniform random endpoints and weights\n2. Selection: select parents with top fitness\n3. Mutation: add scaled direction vectors alongside specular averaging\n4. Reproduction: apply adaptive covariance matrix updates\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n- Coevolve intervals and weights separately with breakpoints updating\n- Use log-transform on weights to avoid infinitesimal mass\n- Bayesian updates of parameter space dimensions\n- Two-level